# Build one complete post feature

Start from the project created in [First product](/docs/getting-started).
`odroe create` finishes by running `odroe init --full-stack`, which generates
one root page with post list and create; it
does not generate a `/posts` route or a detail page. This tutorial first explains
that generated slice, then follows the repository's reference app as the
evolution path: list and create move to `/posts`, and `/posts/[postId]` adds a
typed detail read.

Both shapes keep the same owners visible instead of demonstrating each library
in isolation:

```text
Post, CreatePost, and ListPostsInput records
  → generated typed RPC codecs
  → ServerFunction handlers
  → typed SQL
  → SQLite or D1
  → Query and Mutation
  → Flutter list and create UI
  → reference app detail UI
```

The generated starter can list posts, create one with a database-generated ID,
refresh the collection, and retain it after a native server restart. The
reference app additionally reads that returned ID through its detail route. No
repository, annotation processor, identity map, or handwritten JSON layer is
needed.

## Define the client-safe domain records

Put only cross-runtime data in `lib/posts.dart`:

```dart
typedef Post = ({int id, String title});
typedef CreatePost = ({String title});
typedef ListPostsInput = ({List<int> ids, String sort});
```

This library can be imported by Flutter and by route server files. Keep SQL
drivers, IO, Fetch bindings, and secrets out of it. The record field names and
types become part of the RPC contract.

## Map the complete database row

`lib/posts_database.dart` owns the relational shape. It imports the domain
record but stays independent of a concrete database driver:

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database.dart';

import 'posts.dart';

final class Posts extends SqlTable<Post> {
  Posts() : super('posts');

  late final SqlTableColumn<int> id = column<int>('id', sqlInt);
  late final SqlTableColumn<String> title = column<String>('title', sqlText);

  @override
  late final SqlProjection<Post> projection = SqlProjection<Post>(
    <SqlSelection<Object?>>[id, title],
    (row) => (id: id.read(row, 0), title: title.read(row, 1)),
  );
}

final posts = Posts();
const postQueries = SqlQueries(SqlDialect.sqlite);
```

`posts.title.set(42)` fails analysis. The projection decodes an entire `Post`
instead of letting a `Map<String, dynamic>` escape into the product. This is a
typed relational layer, not an identity-map ORM: schema ownership, SQL
operations, migrations, and resource lifetimes remain visible.

## Add list and create functions

The generated starter declares these functions on its root route and uses
`NoServerInput` for the list. The reference app evolves them under `/posts`,
adds a typed sort and optional ID filter, and imports the same domain source
with a prefix. The route compiler uses that prefix to forward only client-safe
types into `routes.dart`:

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database.dart';
import 'package:odroe/server.dart';

import '../../posts.dart' as models;
import '../../posts_database.dart';
import 'route.dart' as definition;

final route = definition.route.server();

final listPosts = ServerFunction<models.ListPostsInput, List<models.Post>>(
  id: 'posts.list',
  method: HttpMethod.get,
  handler: (context) {
    final input = context.data;
    if (input.ids.length > 100) {
      throw const HttpError(
        400,
        'Post ID filter cannot contain more than 100 values.',
      );
    }
    final order = switch (input.sort) {
      'newest' => posts.id.descending,
      'oldest' => posts.id.ascending,
      _ => throw const HttpError(400, 'Invalid post sort.'),
    };
    return postQueries
        .selectTable(
          posts,
          where: input.ids.isEmpty ? null : posts.id.isIn(input.ids),
          orderBy: <SqlOrder>[order],
        )
        .all(context.request.read(databaseKey));
  },
);

final createPost = ServerFunction<models.CreatePost, models.Post>(
  id: 'posts.create',
  method: HttpMethod.post,
  handler: (context) {
    final title = context.data.title.trim();
    if (title.isEmpty) {
      throw const HttpError(400, 'Post title is required.');
    }
    return postQueries
        .insert(posts, <SqlAssignment>[posts.title.set(title)])
        .returning(posts.projection)
        .one(context.request.read(databaseKey));
  },
);
```

The reference product rejects more than 100 IDs before SQL construction. The
core predicate preserves every candidate and leaves provider parameter budgets
visible to each application instead of silently deduplicating or chunking.

Creation is one statement. The application does not guess the next ID and does
not insert followed by an unrelated select. `one` requires exactly one returned
row; if a driver cannot satisfy that contract, the operation fails visibly.

The reference app then adds a detail route using the same projection. This
route is an evolution example, not a file emitted by `init --full-stack`:

```dart
final readPost = ServerFunction<int, models.Post>(
  id: 'posts.read',
  method: HttpMethod.get,
  handler: (context) async {
    final post = await postQueries
        .selectTable(posts, where: posts.id.equals(context.data))
        .oneOrNull(context.request.read(databaseKey));
    if (post == null) throw const NotFound('Post not found.');
    return post;
  },
);
```

The three explicit IDs are released wire contracts. Changing a variable or
moving a file does not change them. Changing an input or output shape under an
existing ID still requires a coordinated compatibility decision.

## Let Odroe generate the record boundary

Run generation after changing a function or shared record:

```sh
dart run odroe generate
dart analyze --fatal-infos
```

For `CreatePost`, `ListPostsInput`, `Post`, and `List<Post>`, the compiler emits
four symmetric operations: client input encoding, server input decoding,
server output encoding, and client output decoding. Records use field-name
objects on the wire. Collections such as `ListPostsInput.ids` and `List<Post>`
recurse into their element codec.

The generated source remains ordinary Dart and is analyzed with the rest of
the application. Do not edit `lib/routes.dart` or `lib/routes.server.dart`.
Malformed input fails with HTTP 400 before the handler starts. Malformed typed
output becomes `RpcProtocolException` on the client.

See [Typed RPC records](/docs/guides/typed-rpc-records) for supported nested
shapes and compatibility rules.

## Keep database ownership at the host

The native entry opens one process-owned file database before it starts the
listener. It lends that database to requests and closes it with the server:

```dart
FutureOr<Server> createServer() => createNativeServer(
  databasePath:
      Platform.environment['ODROE_SQLITE_PATH'] ?? '.odroe/app.sqlite3',
  migrationsPath:
      Platform.environment['ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH'] ?? 'migrations',
);

Future<Server> createNativeServer({
  required String databasePath,
  String migrationsPath = 'migrations',
}) async {
  if (databasePath.isEmpty) {
    throw ArgumentError.value(databasePath, 'databasePath', 'must not be empty');
  }
  if (migrationsPath.isEmpty) {
    throw ArgumentError.value(
      migrationsPath,
      'migrationsPath',
      'must not be empty',
    );
  }
  final migrations = readSqliteMigrations(migrationsPath);
  final databaseFile = File(databasePath).absolute;
  await databaseFile.parent.create(recursive: true);
  final database = SqliteDatabase.open(databaseFile.path);
  try {
    await database.applyMigrations(migrations);
    return generated.createServer(
      modules: () => <DatabaseModule>[DatabaseModule.borrowed(database)],
      onClose: database.close,
    );
  } on Object {
    await database.close();
    rethrow;
  }
}
```

`readSqliteMigrations` performs the filesystem preflight before the database is
opened. It accepts only top-level regular files named `NNNN_snake_case.sql`,
orders them by numeric version, and rejects duplicate versions. The database
runner then proves that its ledger is an exact prefix of that history before it
executes a pending file.

The starter's initial file uses `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` and a
conflict-ignoring seed so the previous unreleased Native starter, which had the
same table but no ledger, can be adopted without deleting user rows. This is a
specific starter transition; the runner never infers a baseline for arbitrary
existing schemas.

```sql
-- migrations/0002_unify_posts.sql
UPDATE posts
SET title = 'Odroe post 42'
WHERE id = 42 AND title IN ('D1 post 42', 'SQLite post 42');

CREATE INDEX posts_title ON posts(title);
```

After taking a `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` write lock, the runner revalidates the complete
source and applied history. SQLite's own parser then advances statement by
statement instead of Odroe splitting text on semicolons; the complete file and
its ledger row still commit in one transaction. A failure rolls back only that
file. Native stores the exact SQL string and fails if an applied file is edited,
deleted, renamed, or backfilled below a later version. The canonical
`main._odroe_migrations` ledger is reserved and verified before commit. There is
no schema diff, down migration, or generated entity layer. Migration scripts
must not contain transaction or connection control.

`.odroe/app.sqlite3` is ignored local state; production should set
`ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` to a persistent absolute path and
`ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH` to the deployed immutable history when the process
working directory is not the bundle root.

Cloudflare has a different lifetime. Each Fetch invocation wraps its `DB`
binding in `D1SqlDatabase`. Before starting the Worker, explicitly run the
Wrangler migration command against the intended local or remote database;
`cloudflare:dev` does not apply migrations. Native and D1 keep separate ledgers
because they are separate databases, but review the same source history. No
SQLite FFI object or startup migration runner is reachable from the Worker
artifact.

## Read the list with Query

The Flutter page gives each sort order its own cache identity and passes Query's
cancellation signal to the generated RPC reference:

```dart
final QueryKey<List<Post>> listKey =
    QueryKey<List<Post>>('posts.list', <Object?>[sort]);

QueryBuilder<List<Post>>(
  options: QueryOptions<List<Post>>(
    key: listKey,
    query: (query) => generated.routes.posts.listPosts(
      rpc,
      (ids: const <int>[], sort: sort),
      cancelled: query.cancelToken.whenCancelled.then<void>((_) {}),
    ),
  ),
  builder: (context, result) {
    if (!result.hasData && result.isFetching) {
      return const CircularProgressIndicator();
    }
    if (result.isError && !result.hasData) {
      return FilledButton(
        onPressed: () => QueryClientProvider.of(context).invalidateQueries(
          QueryFilter(key: listKey, exact: true),
        ),
        child: const Text('Retry'),
      );
    }
    final posts = result.requireData;
    if (posts.isEmpty) return const Text('No posts yet.');
    return ListView(
      children: <Widget>[
        for (final post in posts) ListTile(title: Text(post.title)),
      ],
    );
  },
)
```

Initial loading, actionable failure, empty data, and loaded data are distinct
states. A retry invalidates this exact sort. Leaving the final observer can
cancel an active list request; it cannot undo a mutation already accepted by
the server. If invalidation fails after a successful create, the starter keeps
the stale list visible, marks it as possibly out of date, and exposes a retry;
it never presents the old list as a successful refresh.

## Create, then invalidate server truth

Keep creation in a mutation lifecycle. Disable duplicate submission and
invalidate every sorted post list only after success:

```dart
MutationBuilder<Post, CreatePost, void>(
  options: MutationOptions<Post, CreatePost, void>(
    key: QueryKey('posts.create'),
    mutation: (input, _) => generated.routes.posts.createPost(rpc, input),
    onSuccess: (_, _, _, mutation) => mutation.client.invalidateQueries(
      QueryFilter(key: QueryKey<Object?>('posts.list')),
    ),
  ),
  builder: (_, state, mutate, _) => FilledButton(
    onPressed: state.isPending
        ? null
        : () => mutate((title: titleController.text)),
    child: Text(state.isPending ? 'Creating…' : 'Create'),
  ),
)
```

The starter does not perform an optimistic insert because it does not own the
database ID. The server-returned `Post` proves the write, and invalidation then
refreshes collection order from server truth.

## Verify native persistence

Run the native development path:

```sh
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
```

Create a post, stop the command, and start it again. The post must remain in the
list because the same `.odroe/app.sqlite3` file is reopened. A restart that
only shows the seed indicates an ownership or path error, not a Query issue.

For an isolated run:

```sh
ODROE_SQLITE_PATH=/tmp/odroe-tutorial.sqlite3 \
  dart run odroe dev --server-only
```

Remove that disposable file yourself when finished. Odroe never deletes a
configured runtime database.

## Verify D1 through local Workerd

Use the project-owned, locked Cloudflare toolchain:

```sh
npm ci
dart run odroe build --no-server --sqlite-migrations migrations web
npm run cloudflare:migrate:local
npm run cloudflare:dev
```

The final command uses Odroe's Cloudflare server-only watcher rather than
starting Wrangler against an unchecked artifact. Initial route generation and
compilation must succeed before Wrangler starts. After startup it watches Dart
server sources and preserves the last-known-good Worker when generation or
compilation fails. Rebuild Flutter Web explicitly after page or asset changes.

The generated starter must list `Odroe post 42`, create a post through D1
`INSERT ... RETURNING`, and list it again. The reference app additionally
filters the list through typed ID membership and reads the created ID through
`posts.read`. An empty title must return controlled HTTP
400 without adding a row. These checks prove the Fetch host,
invocation-scoped D1 adapter, generated record codecs, and typed SQL path. They
do not prove a remote deployment.

## Evolve the feature without hiding ownership

Adding an `excerpt` field is a vertical change:

1. Add it to `Post` and, if accepted from the client, `CreatePost`.
2. Add the typed table column and projection decoder.
3. Append one reviewable SQL migration consumed by both Native and D1.
4. Decide whether the existing RPC IDs remain compatible.
5. Regenerate routes and fix compiler diagnostics.
6. Update Query keys only if resource identity changed.
7. Verify SQLite persistence and local D1 before documenting support.

Do not edit an applied migration, and do not add a repository or universal
entity base class until repeated product code demonstrates the same problem.
The visible path stays reviewable because each layer owns exactly one concern.

Continue with [Database providers](/docs/guides/database-providers),
[Query workflows](/docs/guides/query-workflows), and
[Server operations](/docs/guides/server-operations).
